s\nnD33Kqbk4Z5OZ2QjB0kW1Iywa2+SDS0bQ7NcNCVTUeYhfvadPkQgI6tRwu5I\nJ3nqiao3UPwFXZZoAUxB8VAEPixiaF5sUgDv/3M2/uOgqXMArPn5y2I6UZfc\nLuf6WKzt\n",
    "teacher_markdown": "Misconceptions and what to test for:\r\n\r\n- Museum Ticket is a fresh context on purpose. L10 and L11 stayed inside the movies and Painter examples, and Make is where students apply the pattern somewhere new.\r\n- Rule order is the lesson. A student who puts the under-5 rule before the youth rule gives a 3-year-old $8, because both blocks run and the later one overwrites the earlier. Ages 3 and 4 are the only inputs where two `if` blocks both run (2.3.B.7). In MY TESTING, check that students list both rules for age 3.\r\n- Boundaries: `<` vs `<=`. Age 5 should pay $8, not $0. Age 18 should pay $15, and age 65 should pay $10.\r\n- Setting `price = 15` after the `if` statements resets every visitor to $15. The default has to come first.\r\n- Forgetting `int()` around `input()` causes a TypeError when `age` is compared to a number. Forgetting `str()` when printing the price causes the same error (L25 pattern).\r\n- Hardcoding an age (like `age = 3`) instead of reading it with `input()` fails most checks, because Validate runs the program with many ages.\r\n- Comments that restate the code (\"set price to 8\") instead of explaining purpose (\"the youth price\"). The comment check in MY DOCUMENTATION CHECK addresses this.\r\n- Accept a correct `elif` chain if students bring one in from outside the lesson. It is not yet taught, so don't require it.\r\n- If a student uses Build Python, they still own the program. Ask them to explain each rule aloud, or use Explain Code on a line they didn't write. The comment check also tests this, because a partner has to predict prices from the comments alone.\r\n- Challenge rules: during Validate, every `input()` call receives the age as its answer. A member-discount rule that asks a second question will get the age text, so it should only change the price when the answer is \"yes\" (the age text never matches). A rewrite with `if` and `else` must give the same prices.\r\n\r\nExpected prices: ages 3 and 4 pay $0. Ages 5, 12, and 17 pay $8. Ages 18, 40, and 64 pay $15. Ages 65 and 70 pay $10.\r\n\r\nValidation runs the program with ages 3, 4, 5, 12, 17, 18, 40, 64, 65, and 70. It reads the number after the word \"price\" in the last line that contains it, so a message with another number after \"price\" can be misread.\r\n\r\nTeaching Tip: if a student's price is wrong, suggest printing `price` after each rule so they can see where it changes.\r\n\r\nTeaching Tip: suggested pacing for the 30-minute Make level is about 5 minutes planning, 10 building, 5 validating and testing, 5 for the comment check, and 5 for reflection.",
    "validation_file": {
      "id": "1",
      "name": "test.py",
      "contents": "import io\nimport re\nfrom contextlib import redirect_stdout\nfrom unittest import TestCase\n\n\ndef run_with_age(age):\n    \"\"\"Run the student's main.py as if the visitor typed this age.\"\"\"\n    with open('main.py') as main_file:\n        code = main_file.read()\n    output = io.StringIO()\n    error = None\n    fake_globals = {'input': lambda prompt='': str(age), '__name__': '__main__'}\n    try:\n        with redirect_stdout(output):\n            exec(code, fake_globals)\n    except Exception as e:\n        error = e\n    return output.getvalue().splitlines(), error\n\n\ndef get_price(lines):\n    \"\"\"Find the number after the word 'price' in the last line that has it.\"\"\"\n    for line in reversed(lines):\n        match = re.search(r\"price\\D*(\\d+)\", line.lower())\n        if match:\n            return int(match.group(1))\n    return None\n\n\nclass TestMuseumTicket(TestCase):\n    def check_prices(self, cases, rule_hint):\n        for age, expected in cases:\n            lines, error = run_with_age(age)\n            if error:\n                self.fail(\n                    f\"Your program stopped with a {type(error).__name__} when the visitor's age was {age}. \"\n                    \"Fix that error first. Common causes: leaving out int() around input(), or leaving out str() when you print the price.\"\n                )\n            price = get_price(lines)\n            if price is None:\n                self.skipTest(\"We didn't find a ticket price printed, so we can't check this yet.\")\n            if price != expected:\n                self.fail(f\"With age = {age}, your program printed a price of {price}, but it should be {expected}. {rule_hint}\")\n\n    def test_prints_price(self):\n        \"\"\"Your program prints a ticket price\"\"\"\n        lines, error = run_with_age(40)\n        if error:\n            self.fail(\n                f\"Your program stopped with a {type(error).__name__}. Fix that error first. \"\n                \"Common causes: leaving out int() around input(), or leaving out str() when you print the price.\"\n            )\n        if get_price(lines) is None:\n            self.fail(\"We didn't find a ticket price printed. Print a message that starts with \\\"Ticket price:\\\" followed by the price.\")\n\n    def test_free_rule(self):\n        \"\"\"Kids under 5 get in free\"\"\"\n        self.check_prices([(3, 0), (4, 0)],\n            \"Check the order of your rules: the free rule has to come after the youth rule, so it can override it.\")\n\n    def test_youth_rule(self):\n        \"\"\"Visitors under 18 pay the youth price\"\"\"\n        self.check_prices([(5, 8), (12, 8), (17, 8)],\n            \"Check your youth rule's comparison, and check that the free rule only applies to kids under 5.\")\n\n    def test_senior_rule(self):\n        \"\"\"Visitors 65 and up pay the senior price\"\"\"\n        self.check_prices([(65, 10), (70, 10)],\n            \"Check your senior rule's comparison. A visitor who is exactly 65 counts as 65 and up.\")\n\n    def test_adult_price(self):\n        \"\"\"Everyone else pays the standard adult price\"\"\"\n        self.check_prices([(18, 15), (40, 15), (64, 15)],\n            \"Check that price starts at the adult price, and that your youth and senior rules don't apply to this age.\")",
      "folderId": "0",
      "active": false,
      "type": "validation"
    },
    "long_instructions": "# Make: Build a Museum Ticket Generator\r\n\r\nYour Task: Write a program that asks for a visitor's age and prints the ticket price.\r\n\r\nUse these pricing rules:\r\n\r\n1. Start every visitor at $15.\r\n2. Visitors under 18 pay $8 instead.\r\n3. Visitors 65 and up pay $10 instead.\r\n4. Kids under 5 get in free. This rule goes last because it beats every other rule.\r\n\r\nYour program should:\r\n\r\n- Use an `if` statement for rules 2, 3, and 4, with each condition stored in a clearly named variable.\r\n- Put a comment above each rule that explains its purpose.\r\n- Print `Ticket price:` followed by the price.\r\n\r\nChallenge: Add a member discount rule, or rewrite two of your rules using `if` and `else`.\r\n\r\nDo This:\r\n\r\n1. Fill out MY PLAN in `museum_ticket_plan.txt` before coding.\r\n2. Write your program in `main.py`, then use Validate to check it.\r\n3. Predict the price for each age in MY TESTING, run your program, and record the actual prices.\r\n4. Trade seats with a partner. Your partner reads only your comments and predicts two prices. Record it in MY DOCUMENTATION CHECK, and improve your comments if your partner was wrong.\r\n5. Fill out MY REFLECTION."
  },
